+# Forking Base locally
+
+Our contracts read USDC on Base at `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913`, so the
+local node has to be a **fork of Base**. The onboarding note that says
+
+> Start the chain with `yarn fork base` … Sanity-check it with `cast chain-id` against your
+> local node; if you get a chain id back, the fork is up and you're on Base.
+
+is wrong in both halves. Here is what actually happens in this checkout, and what to run instead.
+
+## 1. Why the token reads come back empty
+
+### `yarn fork base` never forks Base — the `base` argument is dropped
+
+`packages/foundry/package.json` (before the fix in this commit):
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+`${1:-mainnet}` is bash positional-parameter syntax. Yarn 4 does not run scripts through bash —
+it uses its own portable shell, which has **no positional parameters**. `$1` is always empty, so
+`${1:-mainnet}` always expands to the literal default `mainnet`, and the word you typed (`base`)
+is simply appended to the end of the command line, where `make` ignores it as an unknown goal.
+
+Verified in this checkout — `yarn fork base` echoes the recipe it actually runs:
+
+```
+$ yarn fork base
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337      # <-- not base
+```
+
+So `yarn fork base`, `yarn fork optimism`, and plain `yarn fork` are all the same command: a fork
+of **Ethereum mainnet**. On Ethereum mainnet, address `0x8335…2913` has no code (mainnet USDC lives
+at `0xA0b8…eB48`). Every call to it returns empty data, every `balanceOf` reverts or decodes to
+nothing — exactly the "as though the token were not deployed" symptom. The contract is fine; the
+chain is the wrong chain.
+
+Two smaller traps sit behind the same line:
+
+* Trailing `make`-style overrides don't help either — `yarn fork FORK_URL=base` also runs
+  `anvil --fork-url mainnet` (Yarn does not hand that word to `make` as a command-line override).
+* The `mainnet` alias in `packages/foundry/foundry.toml` pointed at the retired Alchemy host
+  `eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io`, which no longer resolves. So on some machines `yarn fork base`
+  doesn't even start: `Error: failed to get fork block number … dns error`. When that happens it is
+  easy to miss, keep talking to whatever node is already on `127.0.0.1:8545` (a plain `yarn chain`
+  anvil, or in one case here a Hardhat node left over from an unrelated project), and conclude
+  "the node answers, transactions mine, so the fork is up". A non-forked node has no tokens at all.
+
+### `cast chain-id` cannot tell you anything about which chain you forked
+
+`packages/foundry/Makefile`:
+
+```make
+# Start a fork
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+The fork target **pins `--chain-id 31337`**. That is deliberate — the frontend and the deploy
+scripts target chain 31337 for local work — but it means the chain id is a constant, not evidence:
+
+| node | `cast chain-id` |
+| --- | --- |
+| `yarn chain` (no fork at all) | `31337` |
+| fork of Ethereum mainnet | `31337` |
+| fork of Base | `31337` |
+
+A real fork of Base would report `8453` only if the `--chain-id` override were absent. So "I got a
+number back, therefore I'm on Base" is a check that cannot fail, and it passed for a node that was
+not a fork of Base. Don't use it.
+
+## 2. The command that actually gives us a fork of Base
+
+`packages/foundry/package.json` now reads:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${FORK_URL:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+Yarn's shell *does* expand environment variables, so pass the chain as one:
+
+```bash
+FORK_URL=base yarn fork
+```
+
+`base` is resolved by Foundry from `[rpc_endpoints]` in `packages/foundry/foundry.toml`:
+
+```toml
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+baseSepolia = "https://sepolia.base.org"
+```
+
+so no URL or API key is needed (`FORK_URL=https://… yarn fork` works too, as does
+`FORK_URL=baseSepolia`). Equivalent if you'd rather skip Yarn:
+
+```bash
+cd packages/foundry && make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+Both run `anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337`, i.e. Base state served on
+`http://127.0.0.1:8545` under chain id 31337. Then `yarn deploy` and `yarn start` as usual.
+
+## 3. The check that would have caught this
+
+Ask the node for something that exists **only** on Base. Two independent reads, both against the
+local node:
+
+```bash
+# 1. Is this an OP-stack chain? The L1Block predeploy exists on Base, not on Ethereum mainnet.
+cast call 0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000015 "number()(uint64)" --rpc-url localhost
+
+# 2. Is the exact USDC we depend on there?
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "symbol()(string)" --rpc-url localhost
+```
+
+Against a fork of Base (run here against a `--fork-url base` anvil):
+
+```
+26035960
+"USDC"
+```
+
+Against a plain `yarn chain` anvil or a fork of Ethereum mainnet, both calls come back empty —
+which is the failure we were chasing, surfaced in one line instead of in our contract.
+
+Check 2 is the one that matters: it asserts the precise address our code reads, so it also rules
+out Optimism or another OP-stack chain (where `0x4200…0015` exists but Base's USDC address does
+not). Check 1 is a useful second opinion when the token read fails and you want to know whether
+you're on the wrong chain or just not forked.
+
+Anvil will also confess directly, which is the fastest triage of all:
+
+```bash
+$ cast rpc anvil_nodeInfo --rpc-url localhost | jq .forkConfig
+{
+  "forkUrl": "https://mainnet.base.org",
+  "forkBlockNumber": 51663174,
+  "forkRetryBackoff": 1000
+}
+```
+
+`"forkUrl": null` means no fork; a different host means the wrong chain. (On a Hardhat node this
+method errors out — itself a sign you're not talking to the anvil you think you are.)
+
+## Changes made in this checkout
+
+* `packages/foundry/package.json` — `fork` script: `${1:-mainnet}` → `${FORK_URL:-mainnet}`, so
+  `FORK_URL=base yarn fork` works instead of silently forking mainnet.
+* `packages/foundry/foundry.toml` — `mainnet` alias moved off the dead `eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io`
+  host to `eth-mainnet.g.alchemy.com`, so the default `yarn fork` starts at all.
+* Please also drop the `cast chain-id` step from the onboarding note; it is pinned to 31337 by the
+  `fork` target and can never detect this problem.
